Then don't let it overwrite the map, but open a nice new window for it
(in the NetHack meaning of "window"). See below.

>     You can always hit <esc> at the --More-- prompt; then you won't
>see any more messages until it's time to provide the next real input.
>At that point, you can use <ctrl/P> to go back through the messages
>which were skipped.  That approach does not work too well if the final
>message is "game over" ;-) or if there are so many skipped that the
>playback buffer can't hold them all.

Maybe you could have a look at the way moria handles this problem. In
moria, the ^P works slightly different: the first time you press it, it
displays the last message on the top line, but when you press it again, it
does not print the one before that message, but it displays all twenty
previous messages. Maybe Nethack could be changed to behave likewise?
For example, a new window could be called that listed all last recalled
messages in reverse order, one per line.

Of course, moria has no solution for the "You die..." message either.

I like Nethack better than Moria, but there are points where Moria is
easier to use...

